      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a4.6.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g4.6.12', 'g4.6.15'],
          lemma: "γέγραπται γὰρ",
          title: "The devil quotes scripture — accurately",
          body: "The second test opens with the same formula Jesus has been using: **“it is written.”** And the citation (Psalm 91:11-12) is fair — no trickery in the wording; the psalm really does promise angelic hands and unbruised feet to the one who shelters in God. What the test does is convert a promise of *protection within* God's way into a dare to *manufacture proof* of it — jump, and force the net to appear. Jesus' reply (Deut 6:16, “You shall not put the Lord to the test”) names the move: demanding that God demonstrate himself on our schedule is not trust but its inversion, with Massah — where Israel demanded “Is the LORD among us or not?” — as the cautionary precedent. It is a scene with an edge for this site's reader: the Gospels never rebuke honest doubt, but they distinguish it from the engineered ultimatum. Thomas asked to *see*; he did not throw himself off anything and demand catching. One of those is called doubt. The other is called testing God — and even the Son declined it.",
        },
      ],
