      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a4.11.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g4.11.8', 'g4.11.11'],
          lemma: "ἄγγελοι … διηκόνουν",
          title: "The angels come anyway",
          body: "The test Jesus refused at the pinnacle — *force the angels to appear* — resolves itself in one quiet clause: the devil leaves, **and angels came and served him**. What could not be extorted arrives unbidden. The verb is διακονέω, the word for waiting at table: after the refusal of magic bread, food served by heaven's own hands. It is a small structural sermon on the difference between demanding a sign and receiving one — and on the order in which they come. The sequence of the three tests is itself an ascent: bread (the body), the temple (religion), the kingdoms (power) — appetite, piety, and politics, the three places where every messianic movement before and since has been bought. Matthew sets the whole ministry after this scene so the reader knows, before a single miracle, what this Messiah has already declined to be.",
        },
      ],
