      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a4.20.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g4.20.3', 'g4.20.4', 'g4.20.7'],
          lemma: "εὐθέως ἀφέντες … ἠκολούθησαν",
          title: "Leaving the nets",
          body: "Twice in three verses: *at once they left* — nets, then boat, then father. Matthew gives no psychology, no prior acquaintance, no deliberation; two sentences from a stranger on the shore, and working men abandon the family business mid-task. Perhaps the scene is compressed (John 1 suggests some of these men had met Jesus before, which softens the strangeness without removing it). But the compression is the point being made: Matthew is not narrating a career decision, he is defining a verb. *Follow* — the word this Gospel will use more than any other for what believing looks like — is presented at its first occurrence as something done before the questions are settled, with the nets still wet. That is worth sitting with if you are the kind of reader who wants the questions settled first (this translator is). The fishermen's questions were not settled; the boat was still right there behind them, and one of them would famously waver on water, deny at a fire, and be reinstated over breakfast. Following did not require finished certainty. It required leaving a net — and the net, note, stayed leavable: they were back fishing in John 21, and were called again. The door, in this Gospel, revolves.",
        },
      ],
